# QMK Repository Rules for Cursor

## Code Organization and Structure

### File Naming and Organization
- Use lowercase with underscores for file names: `my_keyboard.c`, `config.h`
- Place keyboard-specific files in `keyboards/[vendor]/[keyboard]/`
- Use descriptive directory names that match the keyboard manufacturer and model
- Keep keymaps in `keyboards/[vendor]/[keyboard]/keymaps/[keymap_name]/`
- Store shared code in `users/[username]/` for personal libraries

### Keymap Structure
- Always include a `keymap.c` file as the main keymap definition
- Use `config.h` for keymap-specific configuration overrides
- Include `rules.mk` for keymap-specific feature enables
- Add `readme.md` documentation for complex keymaps
- Use descriptive layer names with `enum` declarations

### Code Style
- Follow QMK's coding standards: 4-space indentation, no tabs
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Keep functions under 50 lines when possible
- Comment complex logic and non-obvious implementations
- Use `#pragma once` instead of header guards

## Configuration Management

### Feature Enablement
- Only enable features you actually use in `rules.mk`
- Disable unused features to save memory: `MOUSEKEY_ENABLE = no`
- Use feature-specific config options in `config.h`
- Document why specific features are enabled/disabled
- Check memory usage with `qmk compile -km [keymap] --verbose`

### Hardware Configuration
- Define pin assignments clearly in `config.h`
- Use descriptive names for custom pins: `#define LED_PIN B5`
- Document any non-standard wiring or modifications
- Include electrical specifications in comments
- Test pin assignments on actual hardware

## Development Workflow

### Before Making Changes
- Pull latest changes from QMK master branch
- Create feature branch from master: `git checkout -b feature/my-improvement`
- Verify current setup compiles: `qmk compile -kb [keyboard] -km [keymap]`
- Document the intended change in commit messages
- Check existing issues and PRs for similar work

### Code Changes
- Make atomic commits with single logical changes
- Test each commit individually when possible
- Update documentation when changing behavior
- Add or update comments for modified functions
- Maintain backward compatibility when possible

### Testing Requirements
- Compile test: `qmk compile -kb [keyboard] -km [keymap]`
- Test on actual hardware before committing
- Verify all layers and key combinations work
- Test special features (rotary encoders, RGB, etc.)
- Check memory usage: `qmk compile -kb [keyboard] -km [keymap] --verbose`

## Building and Compilation

### Local Testing
- Always test compile before pushing: `qmk compile -kb [keyboard] -km [keymap]`
- Test with clean build: `qmk clean && qmk compile -kb [keyboard] -km [keymap]`
- Verify no warnings in compilation output
- Test multiple keymaps if changes affect keyboard-level code
- Use `qmk flash` for actual hardware testing

### Memory Management
- Monitor flash usage: aim to stay under 90% capacity
- Monitor RAM usage for complex keymaps
- Use `LTO_ENABLE = yes` for space optimization when needed
- Remove debug code before final commits
- Consider `EXTRAKEY_ENABLE = no` if media keys aren't needed

### Cross-Platform Compatibility
- Test builds on multiple platforms when possible
- Avoid platform-specific code in keyboard definitions
- Use QMK's cross-platform APIs for hardware interaction
- Document any platform-specific requirements
- Test with different toolchain versions

## Quality Assurance

### Code Review Checklist
- All functions have clear purposes and names
- No magic numbers - use defined constants
- Error handling is present where needed
- Memory leaks are prevented
- Code follows QMK conventions

### Documentation Standards
- Update README.md for new keyboards
- Document keymap layouts with visual representations
- Include build instructions and dependencies
- Document any special setup requirements
- Add troubleshooting section for complex setups

### Hardware Testing
- Test all keys register correctly
- Verify modifier combinations work
- Test layer switching and sticky keys
- Confirm RGB/LED functionality if applicable
- Test rotary encoders and special hardware

## Version Control

### Commit Standards
- Use conventional commit format: `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`
- Include keyboard name in commit messages
- Reference issue numbers when applicable
- Keep commits focused on single changes
- Write descriptive commit messages

### Branch Management
- Use descriptive branch names: `keyboards/vendor/model-feature`
- Keep branches focused on single features/fixes
- Rebase onto master before submitting PRs
- Clean up merged branches promptly
- Tag stable releases appropriately

## Debugging and Troubleshooting

### Debug Configuration
- Use `CONSOLE_ENABLE = yes` for debug output
- Add debug prints with `dprintf()` for troubleshooting
- Remove debug code before production commits
- Use `DEBUG_MATRIX_SCAN_RATE` for performance testing
- Enable bootmagic for emergency reset: `BOOTMAGIC_ENABLE = yes`

### Common Issue Prevention
- Always test on actual hardware
- Verify pin assignments match physical wiring
- Check for conflicting key definitions
- Ensure sufficient memory for enabled features
- Test edge cases like rapid key presses

## Performance Optimization

### Efficiency Guidelines
- Minimize matrix scan rate impact
- Use efficient algorithms for custom functions
- Avoid blocking operations in key processing
- Cache computed values when appropriate
- Profile memory usage for complex keymaps

### Resource Management
- Monitor flash memory usage
- Optimize for RAM usage on constrained MCUs
- Use appropriate data types (uint8_t vs uint16_t)
- Minimize global variables
- Use local variables when possible

## Security and Safety

### Safe Practices
- Never commit sensitive information
- Use secure defaults for network features
- Validate user inputs in custom functions
- Avoid buffer overflows in string operations
- Test boundary conditions thoroughly

### Backup and Recovery
- Keep working firmware backup before major changes
- Document recovery procedures for bricked boards
- Test bootloader entry methods
- Maintain known-good configuration
- Document emergency reset procedures